**Types of files in linux:
1. Regular/Normal file: (-)
2. Directory file: (d)
3. Special files:
--> Link file: (l)
--> Block files: (b)
--> Character files:(c)
--> Named pipe files: (p)
--> Socket files: (s) 
syntax:
file <file_name>
--> to check file type
file demo


Link count:
--> Link count represents the numbers of links pointing to a perticular file.

**Inode:
--> stores file metadata such as permission, owner, size, timestamp and other information.
syntax:
ls -li <file_name>
--> to check inode of a file
ls -li demo

Types of links:
1. hard link
2. soft link

1. Hard Link:
--> A hard link is another filename that points to the same data / inode.
syntax:  ln <oginal_file> <link_name>
eg:  ln demo hardlink
--> Both original file and links have same inode number.
--> deleting link does not affect original file or other links
--> deleting or renaming original file does not affect on other links.

2. Soft Link: 
--> A soft link is a shortcut that points to another file.
syntax: ln -s <original_file> <link_name>
eg: ln -s demo softlink
--> Different inode numbers
--> the soft link becomes dangling link (broken link) because its target file is no longer exists.
